{"subjectId":"java","id":"string-methods","title":"Common String Methods & Text Blocks","group":"Core Language: Strings, Arrays & Methods","order":10,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The <code>String</code> API you use daily — searching, slicing, splitting, formatting — plus Java 15 text blocks for multi-line literals.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>Method cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th><th>Example → result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>length()</code></td><td>int</td><td><code>\"hello\".length()</code> → <code>5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>charAt(i)</code></td><td>char</td><td><code>\"hello\".charAt(1)</code> → <code>'e'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>substring(b, e)</code></td><td>String</td><td><code>\"hello\".substring(1, 3)</code> → <code>\"el\"</code> (end exclusive)</td></tr><tr><td><code>indexOf(x)</code> / <code>lastIndexOf(x)</code></td><td>int</td><td><code>-1</code> when not found</td></tr><tr><td><code>contains</code>, <code>startsWith</code>, <code>endsWith</code></td><td>boolean</td><td><code>\"hello\".startsWith(\"he\")</code> → <code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>toUpperCase()</code> / <code>toLowerCase()</code></td><td>String</td><td>new string</td></tr><tr><td><code>trim()</code> / <code>strip()</code></td><td>String</td><td><code>strip()</code> (Java 11) is Unicode-aware</td></tr><tr><td><code>isEmpty()</code> / <code>isBlank()</code></td><td>boolean</td><td><code>\" \".isBlank()</code> → <code>true</code> (Java 11)</td></tr><tr><td><code>replace(a, b)</code></td><td>String</td><td>literal replace of all occurrences</td></tr><tr><td><code>replaceAll(regex, b)</code></td><td>String</td><td>regex-based</td></tr><tr><td><code>split(regex)</code></td><td>String[]</td><td>trailing empty strings removed</td></tr><tr><td><code>toCharArray()</code> / <code>chars()</code></td><td>char[] / IntStream</td><td>iterate characters</td></tr><tr><td><code>compareTo(s)</code></td><td>int</td><td>lexicographic: negative / 0 / positive</td></tr><tr><td><code>repeat(n)</code></td><td>String</td><td><code>\"ab\".repeat(3)</code> → <code>\"ababab\"</code> (Java 11)</td></tr><tr><td><code>String.valueOf(x)</code></td><td>String</td><td>any type → string (<code>\"null\"</code> for null objects)</td></tr><tr><td><code>String.format</code> / <code>formatted</code></td><td>String</td><td>printf-style formatting</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Searching &amp; slicing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s = \"Hello, World\";\n        System.out.println(s.length() + \" \" + s.charAt(7));\n        System.out.println(s.substring(7) + \"|\" + s.substring(0, 5));\n        System.out.println(s.indexOf('o') + \" \" + s.lastIndexOf('o') + \" \" + s.indexOf(\"xyz\"));\n        System.out.println(s.contains(\"World\") + \" \" + s.startsWith(\"Hell\"));\n        System.out.println(s.toLowerCase().replace('l', 'L'));\n        System.out.println(\"  padded  \".strip() + \"|\" + \"   \".isBlank() + \"|\" + \"\".isEmpty());\n        System.out.println(\"apple\".compareTo(\"banana\") + \" \" + \"b\".compareTo(\"a\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Java\".equalsIgnoreCase(\"JAVA\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>12 W\nWorld|Hello\n4 8 -1\ntrue true\nheLLo, worLd\npadded|true|true\n-1 1\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cut\"></i>Splitting &amp; joining</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(\"a,b,,c,,\".split(\",\")));      // trailing empties dropped\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(\"a,b,,c,,\".split(\",\", -1)));  // keep them\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(\"1.2.3\".split(\"\\\\.\")));       // \".\" is a regex!\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(\"  one   two three \".trim().split(\"\\\\s+\")));\n        System.out.println(String.join(\" | \", Arrays.asList(\"x\", \"y\", \"z\")));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[a, b, , c]\n[a, b, , c, , ]\n[1, 2, 3]\n[one, two, three]\nx | y | z</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>split</code> takes a <strong>regex</strong>: <code>split(\".\")</code> returns an empty array because <code>.</code> matches every character. Escape metacharacters: <code>\"\\\\.\"</code>, <code>\"\\\\|\"</code>, or use <code>Pattern.quote(\".\")</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-abc\"></i>Characters</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String word = \"Level42\";\n        int letters = 0, digits = 0;\n        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\n            if (Character.isLetter(c)) letters++;\n            else if (Character.isDigit(c)) digits++;\n        }\n        System.out.println(letters + \" letters, \" + digits + \" digits\");\n        System.out.println((int) 'a' + \" \" + (char) ('a' + 2) + \" \" + ('7' - '0'));\n        System.out.println(new StringBuilder(word).reverse());\n        System.out.println(word.chars().filter(Character::isUpperCase).count());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 letters, 2 digits\n97 c 7\n24leveL\n1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-typography\"></i>Formatting</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(String.format(\"%-6s|%5d|%.2f|%05d\", \"id\", 42, 3.14159, 7));\n        System.out.println(\"%s is %d years\".formatted(\"Asha\", 30));    // Java 15\n        System.out.printf(\"%x %o %e %b%n\", 255, 8, 12345.678, true);\n        System.out.println(String.format(\"%,d\", 1234567));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id    |   42|3.14|00007\nAsha is 30 years\nff 10 1.234568e+04 true\n1,234,567</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-text\"></i>Text blocks (Java 15)</h3>\n<p>A text block starts with <code>\"\"\"</code> followed by a newline. Incidental indentation (the common leading whitespace) is stripped, and line endings are normalised to <code>\\n</code> — ideal for JSON, SQL and HTML.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String json = \"\"\"\n            {\n              \"name\": \"Asha\",\n              \"age\": 30\n            }\n            \"\"\";\n        System.out.print(json);\n        String oneLine = \"\"\"\n            SELECT * \\\n            FROM users\"\"\";                  // \\ joins lines\n        System.out.println(oneLine);\n        System.out.println(\"\"\"\n            Hi %s!\"\"\".formatted(\"Ravi\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{\n  \"name\": \"Asha\",\n  \"age\": 30\n}\nSELECT * FROM users\nHi Ravi!</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Text blocks</div><p>The position of the closing <code>\"\"\"</code> controls indentation: put it on its own line to get a trailing newline; put it after the last text to avoid one.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between trim() and strip()?</summary><div><p><code>trim()</code> removes characters with code point ≤ <code>U+0020</code> (ASCII spaces and control chars). <code>strip()</code> (Java 11) uses <code>Character.isWhitespace</code>, so it also removes Unicode whitespace such as <code>U+2000</code>. <code>stripLeading()</code> / <code>stripTrailing()</code> do one side.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between replace and replaceAll?</summary><div><p>Both replace <em>all</em> occurrences. <code>replace(CharSequence, CharSequence)</code> treats the target literally; <code>replaceAll(String regex, String replacement)</code> interprets a regular expression (and <code>$</code> / <code>\\</code> in the replacement).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you check if a string is a palindrome?</summary><div><p>Compare <code>s</code> with <code>new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString()</code> using <code>equals</code>, or use two indices moving towards the middle — the latter is O(1) extra space.</p></div></details></section>"}